 <h2 class="text-lg font-black uppercase text-amber-700 mb-3">Key Takeaways</h2>
 <ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>IEC 62368-1 is the global safety standard replacing IEC 60065 and IEC 60950</strong>. It uses hazard-based safety engineering (HBSE) covering 5 hazard types: electrical, thermal, mechanical, radiation, chemical. For OEM importers: your charger must maintain case temperature below 65degC at 25degC ambient under 100% load for 4 hours -- this is the single most common certification failure point.</li>
 <li><strong>CE (EU) = EN 62368-1 + EMC + RoHS + WEEE. UL (US) = UL 62368-1 by NRTL</strong>. CE can be self-declared for sub-100W; UL requires third-party certification (UL/ETL/TUV). FCC Part 15 covers EMI. GS (Germany) adds annual factory inspection. Budget $3,000-8,000 per SKU for a full US+EU certification package.</li>
 <li><strong>Creepage distance under 4.0mm between primary and secondary sides = automatic failure</strong>, per IEC 62368-1 Annex M.4 for reinforced insulation at 250V. This is a PCB layout issue, not a component quality issue -- a factory using a reference design from Navitas/Infineon should already meet this. Request the PCB Gerber creepage measurement report before approving samples.</li>
 <li><strong>Pre-compliance testing at the factory saves 3-5 weeks and $2,000-5,000</strong>. A factory with in-house hipot tester, ground bond tester, and thermal chamber catches 80% of failures before the external lab. Ask: "Do you have in-house pre-compliance setup?" If no, budget for 2-3 lab re-test cycles.</li>
 </ul>
 </div>

