 <!-- Section 10: FAQ + Cross-References -->
 <section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

 <!-- FAQ Q&A rendered from schema for human readers -->
 <div class="space-y-4 mb-8">
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue text-sm uppercase mb-2">What safety standards must OEM chargers meet for US and EU markets?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>US:</strong> UL 62368-1, FCC, most retailers require UL or face delisting. <strong>EU:</strong> CE via EN 62368-1 + RoHS + ESPR 2025/2052 (≤0.1W standby, ≥87% efficiency). Key difference: CE = self-declaration with Technical File; UL = NRTL testing + quarterly factory inspections (~$1,500-3,000/year). GaN chargers need same certs + additional thermal testing. WOWOHCOOL: 10-layer protection, UL 94 V-0 enclosures rated to 850°C glow wire across all products.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue text-sm uppercase mb-2">What is the UL 62368-1 safety standard and why does it matter for charger OEM?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">UL 62368-1 covers electric shock, fire, thermal runaway, mechanical hazards, and radiation. <strong>4th Edition (July 2025, mandatory Feb 15, 2027):</strong> all components must be IEC 62368-1 certified (older IEC 60950-1/60065 rejected), adds dual-sensor thermal derating, stricter thin-wall enclosure testing, and single-fault analysis. <strong>New OEM designs should target 4th Edition now</strong> to avoid re-certification costs in 2027.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue text-sm uppercase mb-2">What are the risks of uncertified chargers for OEM brands and importers?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>Yes, US CPSC recalled 447,000+ chargers (2025-2026)</strong>, 112 incidents, 1 fatality. Cheap chargers skip OVP/OCP/OTP, use non-V-0 plastics that ignite. Thermal runaway reaches <strong>800°C within seconds</strong> when internal temp exceeds ~150°C. Prevention: NTC temperature sensors, MCU-controlled cutoff, dual-sensor derating per IEC 62368-1 Ed.3. <strong>One recall costs more than a product line's lifetime savings from cutting corners.</strong></p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue text-sm uppercase mb-2">What safety testing should a quality charger factory perform on every unit?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Minimum: (1) 10-layer circuit protection. (2) <strong>100% 8-hour burn-in at 45°C</strong>, not batch sampling. (3) UL 94 V-0 enclosure rated to 850°C glow wire. (4) IEC 62368-1 Ed.3 dual-sensor thermal derating. (5) 100% hipot testing per unit. GaN: additional thermal imaging at 100% load. WOWOHCOOL: field failure rate <strong>below 0.1%</strong> across millions of units. Red flag: batch-only sampling, no burn-in reports.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue text-sm uppercase mb-2">What is China's GB 47372-2026 safety standard for power banks?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">China's mandatory safety standard under CCC, published March 2026, transition ending <strong>March 31, 2027</strong>. Adds nail-penetration and thermal abuse tests. <strong>~70% of low-end manufacturers expected to exit.</strong> For OEM buyers: verify factory GB 47372-readiness NOW, non-compliant factories face shutdown. WOWOHCOOL is already GB 47372-compliant.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue text-sm uppercase mb-2">How can OEM buyers spot fake or borrowed safety certificates?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Five steps: (1) Verify UL at <strong>iq.ulprospector.com</strong>, must say "UL Listed." (2) Check FCC ID at <strong>fccid.io</strong>. (3) Demand <strong>full test reports (30-80 pages)</strong> from ISO 17025 labs (TÜV, SGS, Intertek, UL), not 1-page certs. (4) Confirm exact model number on report. (5) CE DoC must be in <strong>importer's name</strong>, not factory's. Red flags: PDF-only, no database entry, "family" certs without your SKU, unknown labs.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue text-sm uppercase mb-2">How long do safety certifications last and when do they need renewal?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>UL:</strong> quarterly factory inspections (~$1,500-3,000/yr) + annual recertification. <strong>CE:</strong> update DoC when design/standards change, no fixed expiry but authorities can request docs anytime. <strong>Major transitions</strong> (IEC 62368-1 Ed.3→Ed.4, Feb 2027) = full re-testing. Technical files: retain <strong>10 years</strong> after last unit sold (EU). Penalties: EU fines up to EUR 500,000, US FCC $16,000+/violation/day, Amazon delisting, customs seizure.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue text-sm uppercase mb-2">How do OEM buyers ensure their charger order meets all safety compliance requirements?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>Step 1:</strong> Map target market standards, UL 62368-1 (US), EN 62368-1 + ESPR 2025/2052 (EU), GB 47372-2026 (if China-sourced). <strong>Step 2:</strong> Demand full test reports (30-80 pages) from ISO 17025 labs. <strong>Step 3:</strong> Verify every cert online. <strong>Step 4:</strong> Specify 10-layer protection, V-0 enclosure, 100% burn-in in purchase agreement. <strong>Step 5:</strong> Require CE DoC in your company name. <strong>WOWOHCOOL: complete compliance packs, field failure rate below 0.1%. <a href="/contact/" class="text-brandOrange hover:underline">Request compliance documentation for your OEM project.</a></strong></p>
 </div>
 </div>

 </div>
